// One first-order Trotter step of H = X⊗X + Z⊗I — a stand-in for a real
// interaction term (Ising coupling + local field). This is the actual
// near-term technique behind most chemistry/materials simulation work,
// underneath VQE's more famous ansatz.
//
// exp(-i*t*H) ~= exp(-i*t*X⊗X) * exp(-i*t*Z⊗I) — only exact if the terms
// commuted, which they don't, so real sims use many small steps instead
// of one big one.
//
// theta below must be 2*t, not t — Quell doesn't do arithmetic on PARAM
// references, and RZ(theta) = exp(-i*theta/2*Z).

PARAM theta : angle
qubit a, b

// exp(-i*t*X⊗X): basis-change to Z⊗Z, ZZ rotation, change back. Checked
// against cos(t)|00>-i*sin(t)|11> at t=PI/2: gives |11> at 100%.
H a
H b
CNOT a b
RZ theta b
CNOT a b
H a
H b

// exp(-i*t*Z⊗I): plain RZ on the qubit the Z term acts on.
RZ theta a

MEASURE

// quell run trotter_step.quell --param theta=1.5708  (t = PI/4)
// More, smaller steps approximate exp(-i*t*H) better than one big step.
